{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XLrRVm92WYVUhTbazVjKM5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- gsd:loop-host\n2\tstep: execute\n3\tpoints: execute:pre, execute:wave:pre, execute:wave:post, execute:post\n4\tagent-roles: executor, verifier\n5\tproduces: SUMMARY.md\n6\tconsumes: PLAN.md\n7\t-->\n8\t<purpose>\n9\tExecute all plans in a phase using wave-based parallel execution. Orchestrator stays lean — delegates plan execution to subagents.\n10\t</purpose>\n11\t\n12\t<core_principle>\n13\tOrchestrator coordinates, not executes. Each subagent loads the full execute-plan context. Orchestrator: discover plans → analyze deps → group waves → spawn agents → handle checkpoints → collect results.\n14\t</core_principle>\n15\t\n16\t<!-- #2508 runtime-aware-dispatch -->\n17\t\n18\t> **Runtime-aware dispatch (#2508 Phase 4).** GSD workflows dispatch specialized subagents by role. Before dispatching on a built-in-only runtime (kimi-code — three built-ins only), resolve the role to a built-in via `gsd_run query resolve-dispatch-type --requested <role> --raw`. On named-dispatch runtimes (Claude/OpenCode/…) the role is returned unchanged; on kimi-code it maps to `coder`/`explore`/`plan` by role-suffix. The persona rides `${AGENT_SKILLS_<ROLE>}` (Phase 3) regardless. See @gsd-core/references/runtime-aware-dispatch.md.\n19\t\n20\t<runtime_compatibility>\n21\t**Subagent spawning is runtime-specific:**\n22\t- **Claude Code:** Uses `Agent(subagent_type=\"gsd-executor\", ...)` — backgrounded by default; verify completion\n23\t- **Copilot:** Subagent spawning does not reliably return completion signals. **Default to\n24\t  sequential inline execution**: read and follow execute-plan.md directly for each plan\n25\t  instead of spawning parallel agents. Only attempt parallel spawning if the user\n26\t  explicitly requests it — and in that case, rely on the spot-check fallback in step 3\n27\t  to detect completion.\n28\t- **Codex:** native subagent sessions can end abnormally (`turn_aborted`) after the plan\n29\t  work is already committed. Completion is decided by the step-4 artifact reconciliation\n30\t  (SUMMARY + matching recent commits), not by the session's terminal state (#4217).\n31\t- **Other runtimes:** If `Agent`/`agent` tool is genuinely unavailable (e.g. a backgrounded\n32\t  Claude Code agent per #853, or a non-Claude runtime), use sequential inline execution as\n33\t  the fallback for executor parallelization only. If `Agent` IS available (top-level Claude\n34\t  Code), you MUST spawn gsd-executor agents — inline execution is not authorized. Check for\n35\t  actual tool availability, not runtime name.\n36\t\n37\t**Fallback rule:** If a spawned agent completes its work (commits visible, SUMMARY.md exists) but\n38\tthe orchestrator never receives the completion signal, treat it as successful based on spot-checks\n39\tand continue to the next wave/plan. Never block indefinitely waiting for a signal — always verify\n40\tvia filesystem and git state.\n41\t</runtime_compatibility>\n42\t\n43\t<required_reading>\n44\tRead STATE.md before any operation to load project context.\n45\t@~/.claude/gsd-core/references/agent-contracts.md\n46\t@~/.claude/gsd-core/references/context-budget.md\n47\t@~/.claude/gsd-core/references/gates.md\n48\t</required_reading>\n49\t\n50\t<available_agent_types>\n51\tThese are the valid GSD subagent types registered in .claude/agents/ (or equivalent for your runtime).\n52\tAlways use the exact name from this list — do not fall back to 'general-purpose' or other built-in types:\n53\t\n54\t- gsd-executor — Executes plan tasks, commits, creates SUMMARY.md\n55\t- gsd-verifier — Verifies phase completion, checks quality gates\n56\t- gsd-planner — Creates detailed plans from phase scope\n57\t- gsd-phase-researcher — Researches technical approaches for a phase\n58\t- gsd-plan-checker — Reviews plan quality before execution\n59\t- gsd-debugger — Diagnoses and fixes issues\n60\t- gsd-codebase-mapper — Maps project structure and dependencies\n61\t- gsd-integration-checker — Checks cross-phase integration\n62\t- gsd-nyquist-auditor — Validates verification coverage\n63\t- gsd-ui-researcher — Researches UI/UX approaches\n64\t- gsd-ui-checker — Reviews UI implementation quality\n65\t- gsd-ui-auditor — Audits UI against design requirements\n66\t</available_agent_types>\n67\t\n68\t<process>\n69\t\n70\t**Compact Content Gate.** Read and follow `gsd-core/references/compact-content-gate.md` now — it states the `workflow.compact_content` check and the resolution rule this spine defers to. When it directs a Read, read `gsd-core/workflows/execute-phase/detail/elaboration.md` in full before continuing past this point; its content elaborates on two steps below (check_interactive_mode, cross_ai_delegation).\n71\t\n72\t<step name=\"parse_args\" priority=\"first\">\n73\tParse `$ARGUMENTS` before loading any context:\n74\t\n75\t- First positional token → `PHASE_ARG`\n76\t- Optional `--wave N` → `WAVE_FILTER`\n77\t- Optional `--gaps-only` keeps its current meaning\n78\t- Optional `--cross-ai` → `CROSS_AI_FORCE=true` (force all plans through cross-AI execution)\n79\t- Optional `--no-cross-ai` → `CROSS_AI_DISABLED=true` (disable cross-AI for this run, overrides config and frontmatter)\n80\t\n81\tIf `--wave` is absent, preserve the current behavior of executing all incomplete waves in the phase.\n82\t</step>\n83\t\n84\t<step name=\"initialize\" priority=\"first\">\n85\tLoad all context in one call:\n86\t\n87\t@~/.claude/gsd-core/references/gsd-run-resolver.md\n88\t\n89\t```bash\n90\tWAVE_PARAM=\"\"; if [[ \"$ARGUMENTS\" =~ (^|[[:space:]])--wave[[:space:]]+([^[:space:]-][^[:space:]]*) ]]; then WAVE_PARAM=\"--wave ${BASH_REMATCH[2]}\"; fi\n91\tGSD_WS=$(echo \" $ARGUMENTS\" | sed -nE 's/.* --ws +([A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*).*/--ws \\1/p' | head -n 1)\n92\tINIT=$(gsd_run query init.execute-phase ${GSD_WS:+--ws=${GSD_WS##* }} \"${PHASE_ARG}\" $WAVE_PARAM)\n93\tif [[ \"$INIT\" == @file:* ]]; then INIT=$(cat \"${INIT#@file:}\"); fi\n94\tAGENT_SKILLS=$(gsd_run query agent-skills gsd-executor ${GSD_WS:+--ws=${GSD_WS##* }})\n95\t```\n96\t\n97\tParse JSON for: `executor_model`, `verifier_model`, `commit_docs`, `parallelization`, `branching_strategy`, `branch_name`, `phase_found`, `phase_dir`, `phase_number`, `padded_phase`, `phase_name`, `phase_slug`, `plans`, `incomplete_plans`, `plan_count`, `incomplete_count`, `state_exists`, `roadmap_exists`, `phase_req_ids`, `response_language`, `requirements_path`, `section_manifest`, `threat_id_duplicate_count`.\n98\t\n99\t**Threat-ID gate (#4683):** if `threat_id_duplicate_count` is non-zero, read and execute `execute-phase/steps/threat-id-gate.md` BEFORE any dispatch — it is a hard stop (the full duplicate list is in `threat_id_duplicates`).\n100\t\n101\t`section_manifest` (#2932) gates the three `steps/*.md` reads below: read a step file only when its `id` is in `section_manifest.included` (equivalently, its path is in `section_manifest.read`); skip it — without reading — when its `id` is in `section_manifest.excluded`. When `section_manifest` is `null` (degraded: manifest artifact missing/unreadable), read all three unconditionally — the safe superset.\n102\t\n103\t**Model resolution:** If `executor_model` is `\"inherit\"`, omit the `model=` parameter from all `Agent()` calls — do NOT pass `model=\"inherit\"` to Agent. Omitting the `model=` parameter causes Claude Code to inherit the orchestrator model automatically. Only set `model=` when `executor_model` is an explicit model name (e.g., `\"claude-sonnet-5\"`, `\"claude-opus-4-8\"`).\n104\t\n105\t@~/.claude/gsd-core/references/execute-phase-response-language.md\n106\t\n107\tRead runtime/worktree config and fail closed before any executor dispatch:\n108\t\n109\t```bash\n110\tRUNTIME=$(gsd_run query config-get runtime --default claude --raw 2>/dev/null || echo \"claude\")\n111\tUSE_WORKTREES=$(gsd_run query config-get workflow.use_worktrees --raw 2>/dev/null || echo \"true\")\n112\tEXECUTOR_STALL_INTERVAL_MINUTES=$(gsd_run query config-get executor.stall_detect_interval_minutes --raw 2>/dev/null || echo \"5\")\n113\tEXECUTOR_STALL_THRESHOLD_MINUTES=$(gsd_run query config-get executor.stall_threshold_minutes --raw 2>/dev/null || echo \"10\")\n114\t\n115\t# Resolve ISOLATION + apply its guards: read and execute the \"Resolve ISOLATION\"\n116\t# section of execute-phase/steps/executor-isolation-dispatch.md. It sets\n117\t# ISOLATION (harness-worktree|orchestrator-worktree|none), forces none when\n118\t# USE_WORKTREES=false, fails closed when a host has no primitive, sweeps orphans,\n119\t# and applies the #683 fork-base auto-degrade.\n120\t```\n121\t\n122\t`ISOLATION` — not `RUNTIME` — is the ONLY fan-out branch point; **never add a `RUNTIME = \"codex\"` test here.** Per-host dispatch detail lives in `execute-phase/steps/executor-isolation-dispatch.md` (read from step 3).\n123\t\n124\tIf the project uses git submodules, worktree isolation is unsafe **only when a plan touches a submodule path** — the executor commit protocol cannot correctly handle submodule commits inside isolated worktrees. Compute submodule paths once and intersect them per-plan with the plan's declared `files_modified` frontmatter.\n125\t\n126\t```bash\n127\t# Parse submodule paths from .gitmodules once (empty if no .gitmodules).\n128\t# SUBMODULE_PATHS is a newline-separated list of repo-relative paths.\n129\tif [ -f .gitmodules ]; then\n130\t  SUBMODULE_PATHS=$(git config --file .gitmodules --get-regexp '^submodule\\..*\\.path$' 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $2}')\n131\telse\n132\t  SUBMODULE_PATHS=\"\"\n133\tfi\n134\t```\n135\t\n136\t`SUBMODULE_PATHS` is exported to the `execute_waves` step, where the per-plan decision happens (see \"Per-plan worktree decision\" sub-step inside `execute_waves`). The decision is per-plan because different plans in the same wave can touch different files — only plans whose paths intersect a submodule must drop worktree isolation; plans nowhere near a submodule keep parallel isolation.\n137\t\n138\tWhen `USE_WORKTREES` is `false`, `ISOLATION` is forced to `none`: executors run sequentially on the main working tree. The per-plan decision below has no effect when worktrees are project-disabled.\n139\t\n140\t`USE_WORKTREES` and `ISOLATION` are also reset for the run when `worktree base-check` detects the orchestrator HEAD has diverged from the worktree fork base (#683) — read and follow `execute-phase/steps/worktree-base-check.md` for the degrade semantics and the `worktree.baseRef:\"head\"` escape hatch (#3659).\n141\t\n142\tRead context window size for adaptive prompt enrichment:\n143\t\n144\t```bash\n145\tCONTEXT_WINDOW=$(gsd_run query config-get context_window --raw 2>/dev/null || echo \"200000\")\n146\t```\n147\t\n148\tWhen `CONTEXT_WINDOW >= 500000` (1M-class models), subagent prompts include richer context:\n149\t- Executor agents receive prior wave SUMMARY.md files and the phase CONTEXT.md/RESEARCH.md\n150\t- Verifier agents receive all PLAN.md, SUMMARY.md, CONTEXT.md files plus REQUIREMENTS.md\n151\t- This enables cross-phase awareness and history-aware verification\n152\t\n153\tWhen `CONTEXT_WINDOW < 200000` (sub-200K models), subagent prompts are thinned to reduce static overhead:\n154\t- Executor agents omit extended deviation rule examples and checkpoint examples from inline prompt — load on-demand via @~/.claude/gsd-core/references/executor-examples.md\n155\t- Planner agents omit extended anti-pattern lists and specificity examples from inline prompt — load on-demand via @~/.claude/gsd-core/references/planner-antipatterns.md\n156\t- Core rules and decision logic remain inline; only verbose examples and edge-case lists are extracted\n157\t- This reduces executor static overhead by ~40% while preserving behavioral correctness\n158\t\n159\t**If `phase_found` is false:** Error — phase directory not found.\n160\t**If `plan_count` is 0:** Error — no plans found in phase.\n161\t**If `state_exists` is false but `.planning/` exists:** Offer reconstruct or continue.\n162\t\n163\tWhen `parallelization` is false, plans within a wave execute sequentially.\n164\t\n165\t**Runtime detection for Copilot:**\n166\tCheck if the current runtime is Copilot by testing for the `@gsd-executor` agent pattern\n167\tor absence of the `Agent()` subagent API. If running under Copilot, force sequential inline\n168\texecution regardless of the `parallelization` setting — Copilot's subagent completion\n169\tsignals are unreliable (see `<runtime_compatibility>`). Set `COPILOT_SEQUENTIAL=true`\n170\tinternally and skip the `execute_waves` step in favor of `check_interactive_mode`'s\n171\tinline path for each plan.\n172\t\n173\t**REQUIRED — Sync chain flag with intent.** If user invoked manually (no `--auto`), clear the ephemeral chain flag from any previous interrupted `--auto` chain. This prevents stale `_auto_chain_active: true` from causing unwanted auto-advance. This does NOT touch `workflow.auto_advance` (the user's persistent settings preference). You MUST execute this bash block before any config reads:\n174\t```bash\n175\t# REQUIRED: prevents stale auto-chain from previous --auto runs\n176\tif [[ ! \"$ARGUMENTS\" =~ --auto ]]; then\n177\t  gsd_run query config-set workflow._auto_chain_active false || true\n178\tfi\n179\t```\n180\t\n181\tResolve `MVP_MODE` once via the centralized `phase.mvp-mode` query verb (precedence chain: CLI flag → ROADMAP `**Mode:** mvp` → `workflow.mvp_mode` config → false):\n182\t```bash\n183\tMVP_FLAG_ARG=\"\"\n184\tif [[ \"$ARGUMENTS\" =~ (^|[[:space:]])--mvp([[:space:]]|$) ]]; then MVP_FLAG_ARG=\"--cli-flag\"; fi\n185\tMVP_MODE=$(gsd_run query phase.mvp-mode \"${PHASE_NUMBER}\" $MVP_FLAG_ARG --pick active)\n186\tEXECUTE_POST_HOOKS_JSON=$(gsd_run loop render-hooks execute:post --raw)\n187\tTDD_MODE=$(gsd_run loop render-hooks execute:post --active-cap tdd)\n188\t```\n189\t\n190\t<step name=\"safe_resume_gate\">\n191\tBefore trusting `STATE.md` or dispatching any executor, derive `CURRENT_PLAN_ID`\n192\tfrom the active incomplete plan in `INIT`, then search recent history:\n193\t```bash\n194\tSUMMARY_PATH=\"{phase_dir}/{plan_padded}-SUMMARY.md\"\n195\t# #5164: the plan's commits come from the evaluation-scope resolver (ADR-5057 §4): anchored\n196\t# on the commit SUBJECT, zero-pad tolerant (#4003) and tolerant of a decimal / N-segment /\n197\t# letter-suffixed phase number (#4619, #4748), reachable from THIS branch only, bounded to\n198\t# the latest reachable tag (milestone marker). One `<sha> <subject>` line per commit, newest first.\n199\tPHASE_NUMBER=\"{phase_number}\"\n200\tPLAN_COMMITS=$(gsd_run check evaluation-scope --plan \"${PHASE_NUMBER}-{plan_padded}\" --commits-only --milestone-bound --max-commits 30 --raw 2>/dev/null) && PLAN_SCOPE_RC=0 || PLAN_SCOPE_RC=$?\n201\t# Exit 69 (UNAVAILABLE) = \"could not look\": empty is then NOT \"no commits\". Fail closed (#5170).\n202\tif [ \"$PLAN_SCOPE_RC\" -ne 0 ]; then echo \"SAFE-RESUME GATE: could not resolve the plan's commits (evaluation-scope exit ${PLAN_SCOPE_RC}); not dispatching an executor on an unknown resume state.\" >&2; exit 1; fi\n203\tPLAN_COMMITS=$(printf '%s' \"$PLAN_COMMITS\" | node -e \"let s='';process.stdin.on('data',d=>s+=d).on('end',()=>{try{process.stdout.write(JSON.parse(s).commits.map(c=>c.sha.slice(0,10)+' '+c.subject).join('\\n'))}catch{}})\")\n204\t```\n205\tIf production commits exist and `SUMMARY.md is missing` (no `.planning/async-jobs/*.json` manifest matches it: a match is a legal `external_job_waiting` deferral - reconcile per `docs/reference/planning-artifacts.md`, never re-dispatch), stop before spawning a\n206\tnew executor; continuing risks duplicate work and stale `STATE.md`/ROADMAP progress.\n207\tOffer these recovery options:\n208\t- `close out manually` — inspect commits, write SUMMARY.md, then update STATE/ROADMAP.\n209\t- `re-execute from scratch` — revert or supersede partial commits before dispatch.\n210\t- `mark-and-skip` — record the anomaly and move on only with explicit confirmation.\n211\t</step>\n212\t\n213\t**TDD gate.** Task-scoped enforcement runs inside plan execution (immediately before each implementation step), where `TASK_FILE`, `PLAN_ID`, and `TASK_ID` are defined. #4011: the gate keys on `TDD_MODE` ALONE — a discipline gate coupled to the product-scope `MVP_MODE` flag was silently inert on every non-MVP phase, contradicting `gsd-core/references/tdd.md`'s contract that `workflow.tdd_mode` binds for all `type: tdd` plans. MVP mode remains free to imply TDD; it is no longer required by it. Keep the same predicate and RED-commit contract:\n214\t```bash\n215\tif [ \"$TDD_MODE\" = \"true\" ]; then\n216\t  IS_BEHAVIOR_ADDING=$(gsd_run query task.is-behavior-adding \"$TASK_FILE\" --pick is_behavior_adding)\n217\t  if [ \"$IS_BEHAVIOR_ADDING\" = \"true\" ]; then\n218\t    # #5164: the same resolver, scope and milestone bound as safe_resume_gate — anchored,\n219\t    # zero-pad tolerant (#4003, #4619, #4748), this branch only. A padded literal grep\n220\t    # hard-halted on a correct unpadded RED commit.\n221\t    # #4379: this pathspec IS the gate's definition of \"a test file\". It was\n222\t    # JS/TS-only, so Go tripped on every task; and `**/` never matches a\n223\t    # root-level path, so a root `foo.test.js` was invisible too. Bare globs\n224\t    # match at every depth. NOT widened to ordinary source — that would make\n225\t    # the gate pass on any in-scope commit. Trade-offs and the Rust gap:\n226\t    # references/tdd.md § Create first test file.\n227\t\n228\t    RED_COMMIT=$(gsd_run check evaluation-scope --plan \"${PHASE_NUMBER}-${PLAN_ID}\" --commits-only --milestone-bound --max-commits 1 --raw --pathspec \"*.test.*\" --pathspec \"*.spec.*\" --pathspec \"tests/\" --pathspec \"__tests__/\" --pathspec \"*_test.go\" --pathspec \"test_*.py\" --pathspec \"*_test.py\" --pathspec \"*_test.exs\" --pathspec \"*_spec.rb\" --pathspec \"*_test.rb\" 2>/dev/null) && RED_SCOPE_RC=0 || RED_SCOPE_RC=$?\n229\t    # Exit 69 (UNAVAILABLE) = the scope could not be resolved: that is not \"missing RED commit\" (#5170).\n230\t    if [ \"$RED_SCOPE_RC\" -ne 0 ]; then echo \"TDD GATE UNAVAILABLE: could not resolve the plan's commits for ${PLAN_ID}/${TASK_ID} (evaluation-scope exit ${RED_SCOPE_RC})\"; exit 1; fi\n231\t    RED_COMMIT=$(printf '%s' \"$RED_COMMIT\" | node -e \"let s='';process.stdin.on('data',d=>s+=d).on('end',()=>{try{const c=JSON.parse(s).commits;process.stdout.write(c.length?c[0].sha.slice(0,10)+' '+c[0].subject:'')}catch{}})\")\n232\t    if [ -z \"$RED_COMMIT\" ]; then\n233\t      gsd_run query state.update last_gate_trip \"${PLAN_ID}/${TASK_ID}\" || true\n234\t      echo \"TDD GATE TRIPPED: missing RED commit for ${PLAN_ID}/${TASK_ID}\"\n235\t      exit 1\n236\t    fi\n237\t  fi\n238\tfi\n239\t```\n240\tPure doc-only / config-only / test-only tasks return `is_behavior_adding=false` and are exempt. When the gate trips, Read `$HOME/.claude/gsd-core/references/execute-mvp-tdd.md` for the exact halt report format.\n241\t</step>\n242\t\n243\t<step name=\"check_blocking_antipatterns\" priority=\"first\">\n244\t**MANDATORY — Check for blocking anti-patterns before any other work.**\n245\t\n246\tLook for a `.continue-here.md` in the current phase directory:\n247\t\n248\t```bash\n249\tls ${phase_dir}/.continue-here.md 2>/dev/null || true\n250\t```\n251\t\n252\tIf `.continue-here.md` exists, parse its \"Critical Anti-Patterns\" table for rows with `severity` = `blocking`.\n253\t\n254\t**If one or more `blocking` anti-patterns are found:**\n255\t\n256\tThis step cannot be skipped. Before proceeding to `check_interactive_mode` or any other step, the agent must demonstrate understanding of each blocking anti-pattern by answering all three questions for each one:\n257\t\n258\t1. **What is this anti-pattern?** — Describe it in your own words, not by quoting the handoff.\n259\t2. **How did it manifest?** — Explain the specific failure that caused it to be recorded.\n260\t3. **What structural mechanism (not acknowledgment) prevents it?** — Name the concrete step, checklist item, or enforcement mechanism that stops recurrence.\n261\t\n262\tWrite these answers inline before continuing. If a blocking anti-pattern cannot be answered from the context in `.continue-here.md`, stop and ask the user for clarification.\n263\t\n264\t**If no `.continue-here.md` exists, or no `blocking` rows are found:** Proceed directly to `check_interactive_mode`.\n265\t</step>\n266\t\n267\t<step name=\"check_interactive_mode\">\n268\t**Parse `--interactive` flag from $ARGUMENTS.** If present, switch to interactive execution mode: plans run sequentially **inline** (no subagent spawning, ignoring wave grouping), reading `execute-plan.md` directly rather than dispatching `gsd-executor`. **Once per plan** (not per task), present a 4-option menu (execute / review-first / skip / stop) before starting that plan's tasks. Once executing, tasks run one at a time with only a brief pause after each — the agent stops mid-plan only if the user actually types something, it does not re-show the menu. After all plans, proceed to verification as normal. Full flow (the exact presentation format, the review-first sub-branch): `gsd-core/workflows/execute-phase/detail/elaboration.md` § 1.\n269\t\n270\t**Skip to handle_branching step** (interactive plans execute inline after grouping).\n271\t</step>\n272\t\n273\t<step name=\"handle_branching\">\n274\tCheck `branching_strategy` from init:\n275\t\n276\t**\"none\":** Read and execute `execute-phase/steps/protected-branch.md`.\n277\t\n278\t**\"phase\" or \"milestone\":** Use pre-computed `branch_name` from init.\n279\t\n280\tFork the new phase branch off `origin/HEAD` (the project's default branch), not the current HEAD — otherwise consecutive phases compound and stay unpushed (#2916). If `$BRANCH_NAME` already exists locally, reuse it as-is.\n281\t\n282\t```bash\n283\tDEFAULT_BRANCH=$(gsd_run query git.base-branch 2>/dev/null \\\n284\t  || git symbolic-ref --quiet --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD 2>/dev/null | sed 's|^origin/||' \\\n285\t  || echo main)\n286\t\n287\tif git show-ref --verify --quiet \"refs/heads/$BRANCH_NAME\"; then\n288\t  git switch \"$BRANCH_NAME\" || { echo \"ERROR: Could not switch to existing branch '$BRANCH_NAME'.\" >&2; exit 1; }\n289\telse\n290\t  if ! git fetch --quiet origin \"$DEFAULT_BRANCH\"; then  # #2916\n291\t    git show-ref --verify --quiet \"refs/remotes/origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH\" \\\n292\t      || { echo \"ERROR: fetch origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH failed and no local copy exists. Refusing to create '$BRANCH_NAME' off current HEAD (#2916).\" >&2; exit 1; }\n293\t    echo \"WARNING: fetch origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH failed; using local copy as base.\" >&2\n294\t  fi\n295\t  if [ -n \"$(git status --porcelain)\" ]; then\n296\t    echo \"WARNING: Uncommitted changes will be carried onto '$BRANCH_NAME' (branched off origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH, not previous HEAD).\"\n297\t  else\n298\t    git switch --quiet \"$DEFAULT_BRANCH\" 2>/dev/null && git merge --ff-only --quiet \"origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH\" 2>/dev/null || true\n299\t  fi\n300\t  # Pinned base (#2916); --no-track (#2498). #2639: warn if local ahead of origin.\n301\t  AHEAD=$(git rev-list --count \"origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH..$DEFAULT_BRANCH\" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)\n302\t  [ \"$AHEAD\" != \"0\" ] && [ -n \"$AHEAD\" ] && echo \"WARNING: $DEFAULT_BRANCH is $AHEAD ahead of origin — '$BRANCH_NAME' won't include those commits (#2639).\" >&2\n303\t  git checkout -b \"$BRANCH_NAME\" \"origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH\" --no-track \\\n304\t    || { echo \"ERROR: Could not create '$BRANCH_NAME' from origin/$DEFAULT_BRANCH (#2916).\" >&2; exit 1; }\n305\tfi\n306\t```\n307\t\n308\tAll subsequent commits go to this branch. User handles merging.\n309\t</step>\n310\t\n311\t<step name=\"validate_phase\">\n312\tFrom init JSON: `phase_dir`, `plan_count`, `incomplete_count`.\n313\t\n314\tReport: \"Found {plan_count} plans in {phase_dir} ({incomplete_count} incomplete)\"\n315\t\n316\t**Update STATE.md for phase start:**\n317\t```bash\n318\tgsd_run query state.begin-phase --phase \"${PHASE_NUMBER}\" --name \"${PHASE_NAME}\" --plans \"${PLAN_COUNT}\"\n319\t```\n320\tThis updates Status, Last Activity, Current focus, Current Position, and plan counts in STATE.md so frontmatter and body text reflect the active phase immediately.\n321\t</step>\n322\t\n323\t<step name=\"discover_and_group_plans\">\n324\tLoad plan inventory with wave grouping in one call:\n325\t\n326\t```bash\n327\tPLAN_INDEX=$(gsd_run query phase-plan-index \"${PHASE_NUMBER}\")\n328\t```\n329\t\n330\tParse JSON for: `phase`, `plans[]`, `waves`, `incomplete`, `runnable`, `ready_plans`, `has_checkpoints` — full per-plan fields and the #4628 readiness rules: read and follow `execute-phase/steps/ready-wave-gate.md`.\n331\t\n332\t**Filtering:** Skip plans where `has_summary: true`. Additionally skip any plan whose `blocked_by` array is non-empty (#2830) — it depends, directly or transitively, on a plan that halted at a designed stop rather than completing — and report it by name: \"Skipping {plan.id}: blocked by halted {blocked_by.join(', ')}\". Never silently drop a blocked plan from the report; it must appear by name with its reason, not merely vanish from the executable list. This rule is additive to the `has_summary` skip, not a replacement for it. Additionally skip any incomplete plan with `ready: false` (#4628 — see `execute-phase/steps/ready-wave-gate.md`; never dispatch a not-ready plan). If `--gaps-only`: also skip plans whose `gap_closure` is not `true` — but first confirm every plan object carries a `gap_closure` key. If any lacks it, the index cannot answer the filter (an older gsd-tools): STOP and report \"--gaps-only cannot read gap_closure from phase-plan-index — refusing to report an empty selection\" rather than filtering. A key the index never emitted must not surface as \"No matching incomplete plans\" (#4924). If `WAVE_FILTER` is set: also skip plans whose `wave` does not equal `WAVE_FILTER`.\n333\t\n334\t**Wave safety check:** If `WAVE_FILTER` is set and there are still incomplete plans in any lower wave that match the current execution mode, STOP and tell the user to finish earlier waves first. Do not let Wave 2+ execute while prerequisite earlier-wave plans remain incomplete.\n335\t\n336\t**If all filtered — do NOT exit unconditionally (#2868).** \"No plan work left\" and \"phase fully\n337\tdone\" are different conditions: a run can be interrupted between the final wave's SUMMARY and\n338\t`verify_phase_goal` (most commonly by a checkpoint plan that is retired but still writes a SUMMARY),\n339\tleaving a phase that looks complete from every index yet never produced `*-VERIFICATION.md`. A\n340\tthird condition looks identical to the first two by plan_count alone but is neither: some filtered\n341\tplans were filtered because they are **blocked** (non-empty `blocked_by`, #2830), not because they\n342\tare done. Blocked-and-incomplete must never be reported as finished.\n343\t\n344\t```bash\n345\t# #5118: the owner's answer — `status` and the bare command it routes to. A non-zero exit\n346\t# (an out-of-set report status) is a hard error: surface it, never read it as \"no status\".\n347\tVERIFY_STATUS=$(gsd_run query verification status \"${PHASE_DIR}\" --pick status) || { echo \"verification status failed — see the error above\" >&2; exit 1; }\n348\tVERIFY_ROUTE=$(gsd_run query verification status \"${PHASE_DIR}\" --pick route) || { echo \"verification status failed — see the error above\" >&2; exit 1; }\n349\t# #3684: checkbox = marked-complete; report fields can claim a no-op write (#3685).\n350\tANALYZE=$(gsd_run query roadmap.analyze)\n351\tif [[ \"$ANALYZE\" == @file:* ]]; then ANALYZE=$(cat \"${ANALYZE#@file:}\"); fi\n352\tPHASE_MARKED=$(echo \"$ANALYZE\"|jq -r --arg p \"$PHASE_NUMBER\" 'def n:sub(\"^0+(?=[0-9])\";\"\");.phases[]|select(((.number//.phase_number|tostring|n))==($p|n))|.roadmap_complete'|head -1)\n353\t```\n354\t\n355\tEvaluate in this exact order — the first matching condition decides the outcome; do not evaluate\n356\tlater conditions once one matches:\n357\t\n358\t1. **A filter is active** (`--gaps-only`, or `WAVE_FILTER` set): report \"No matching incomplete\n359\t   plans\" → exit, unchanged. A filtered run finding nothing left in ITS slice says nothing about\n360\t   whether the phase as a whole is done, and must never jump to verification.\n361\t2. **No filter is active, and at least one filtered plan was skipped because of a non-empty\n362\t   `blocked_by`** (irrespective of `VERIFY_STATUS`): the phase is NOT finished — it is **stuck on a\n363\t   halt**. A plan with no SUMMARY and no dispatched work must never be treated as done merely\n364\t   because nothing was left to filter. Report:\n365\t   `\"Phase stuck: {blocked plan ids} blocked by halted {their blocked_by ids} — resolve the halt, do not resume verification.\"`\n366\t   → exit. Do not fall through to condition 3; this is not a completion state.\n367\t2b. **No filter is active, no blocked-plan skip occurred, and at least one filtered plan was skipped because `ready: false` (#4628)** — the phase is WAITING on incomplete predecessors, not finished: report it by name and exit before any completion state (`execute-phase/steps/ready-wave-gate.md`).\n368\t3. **No filter is active, and every filtered plan was filtered by `has_summary` alone** (no\n369\t   blocked-plan skip occurred) — branch on the owner's answer, never on a status word (#5118):\n370\t   - **`VERIFY_ROUTE == execute-phase`** — the owner routes here when the verify step never ran\n371\t     (`VERIFY_STATUS == missing`, #2868) and when covered source changed after the verifier ran\n372\t     (#4682); on both sides of `PHASE_MARKED`. Report `$VERIFY_STATUS` with:\n373\t     `\"All {plan_count} plans are summarized but no VERIFICATION.md exists — resuming at the phase gates (#2868).\"`\n374\t     (or, for a stale report, \"…verification is stale — resuming at the phase gates to re-run the verifier (#4682).\")\n375\t     SKIP `cross_ai_delegation`, `execute_waves` and `checkpoint_handling` — there is no wave work\n376\t     to do — and continue directly at `aggregate_results`, NOT `code_review_gate`. `aggregate_results`\n377\t     reads exclusively from on-disk `${PHASE_DIR}` artifacts (`*-SUMMARY.md`) and independent\n378\t     `gsd_run` calls — nothing it reads is produced only by `execute_waves` or `checkpoint_handling` —\n379\t     so it tolerates having executed no plans in this run; the `SECURITY_FILE` / secure-phase\n380\t     threats-open gate is the shared verification step's first step, and it reads on-disk artifacts\n381\t     only too. From there the run proceeds exactly as a normal one: `aggregate_results` →\n382\t     `verify_phase_goal` (the shared verification step: `verify_post_security_gate` →\n383\t     `code_review_gate` → `close_parent_artifacts` → `regression_gate` → verifier) →\n384\t     `update_roadmap`. Never skip `aggregate_results`, `code_review_gate` or `regression_gate` on\n385\t     this path — the manual workaround this replaces skipped all three, and that gap is the reason\n386\t     this route exists rather than telling users to spawn the verifier by hand.\n387\t   - **`VERIFY_STATUS == passed` + `PHASE_MARKED` is `true`**: genuinely finished.\n388\t     Report \"No matching incomplete plans\" → exit, unchanged.\n389\t   - **`VERIFY_STATUS == passed` + `PHASE_MARKED` not `true`** — the run died between\n390\t     `verify_phase_goal` and `update_roadmap` (#3684): verification EXISTS — do not redo\n391\t     it or the gates already run. Report `\"Phase {X} is verified but never marked\n392\t     complete — resuming at update_roadmap (#3684).\"` and continue directly at\n393\t     `update_roadmap`; the tail steps then run in their normal order. This is the ONLY arm that\n394\t     reaches `update_roadmap`.\n395\t   - **Anything else** (`gaps_found`, `human_needed`, `unparseable`, `phase_dir_not_found`):\n396\t     present the owner's `next_action` and `next_command` (`gsd_run query verification status\n397\t     \"${PHASE_DIR}\" --pick next_action` / `--pick next_command`) → exit. Never `update_roadmap`.\n398\t\n399\tReport:\n400\t```\n401\t## Execution Plan\n402\t\n403\t**Phase {X}: {Name}** — {total_plans} matching plans across {wave_count} wave(s)\n404\t\n405\t{If WAVE_FILTER is set: `Wave filter active: executing only Wave {WAVE_FILTER}`.}\n406\t\n407\t| Wave | Plans | What it builds |\n408\t|------|-------|----------------|\n409\t| 1 | 01-01, 01-02 | {from plan objectives, 3-8 words} |\n410\t| 2 | 01-03 | ... |\n411\t```\n412\t</step>\n413\t\n414\t<step name=\"cross_ai_delegation\">\n415\t**Optional step 2.5 — Delegate plans to an external AI runtime.** Runs after plan discovery, before wave execution. Activates when `--cross-ai` forces all incomplete plans, `--no-cross-ai` disables it entirely, or (default) a plan's `cross_ai: true` frontmatter agrees with the `workflow.cross_ai_execution` config. **Only `ready` plans are eligible (#4628) — a not-ready plan is never delegated over incomplete predecessors.** If no plan is marked, skip to execute_waves; if marked but `workflow.cross_ai_command` is unset, error and tell the user to set it.\n416\t\n417\tFor each marked plan: build a self-contained prompt from the plan's `<objective>`/`<tasks>` plus PROJECT.md context, warn on a dirty working tree, then run the configured command **wrapped in `gsd_run run-with-timeout \"${CROSS_AI_TIMEOUT}\"` (config `workflow.cross_ai_timeout`, default 300s) — never run it unbounded** — with the prompt piped to **stdin, never shell-interpolated, to prevent injection**. On success (exit 0): validate the captured SUMMARY output is non-empty and structurally valid before writing it as the plan's SUMMARY.md, update STATE/ROADMAP, mark handled. On failure (non-zero exit, or the summary fails that validation): show the error, warn about possible partial edits, and offer **retry** / **skip** (falls back to the normal executor) / **abort**. Successfully handled plans are removed from execute_waves' list; skipped-to-fallback plans remain in it.\n418\t\n419\tExact bash and per-branch wording: `gsd-core/workflows/execute-phase/detail/elaboration.md` § 2.\n420\t</step>\n421\t\n422\t<step name=\"execute_waves\">\n423\tExecute each selected wave in sequence. Within a wave: parallel if `PARALLELIZATION=true`, sequential if `false`.\n424\t\n425\t**Orchestrator cwd-drift guard (FIRST ACTION at execute_waves entry — #48):**\n426\t\n427\tA prior `Agent(isolation=\"worktree\")` dispatch can silently leave the orchestrator's\n428\tcwd inside an agent worktree (or a subdirectory of one). Every subsequent\n429\torchestrator-side git call would then target the wrong tree — this is how a wrong-base\n430\tmerge nearly shipped ~1000 files. Resolve the *worktree root* (so a subdirectory cwd\n431\tcannot skew the check) and refuse if it is an agent worktree. The discriminator is the\n432\tper-agent branch namespace `agent-`/`worktree-agent-`/`worktree-wf_`, NOT the path: the\n433\torchestrator may itself be legitimately invoked from a feature worktree under\n434\t`.claude/worktrees/`, so a path-substring refusal would break legitimate runs. Do NOT\n435\tpin to `git worktree list`'s first entry — that is the main worktree, the wrong target\n436\twhen the orchestrator legitimately runs from a feature worktree.\n437\t\n438\t```bash\n439\t# gsd:guard=orchestrator-cwd-drift\n440\tORCHESTRATOR_WT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || {\n441\t  echo \"FATAL: execute_waves entry is not inside a git worktree (#48).\" >&2; exit 1; }\n442\tORCH_BRANCH=$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null)\n443\tif printf '%s' \"$ORCH_BRANCH\" | grep -Eq '^((worktree-)?agent-|worktree-wf_)'; then\n444\t  echo \"FATAL: orchestrator cwd is inside an agent worktree (branch '$ORCH_BRANCH', root '$ORCHESTRATOR_WT') — refusing to execute waves (#48). A prior isolation=\\\"worktree\\\" dispatch drifted the cwd; re-run from the orchestrator's own worktree.\" >&2\n445\t  # #1856 handoff: the refusal above is correct, but on its own it is a dead end —\n446\t  # this worktree may hold committed fixes AND uncommitted work, and \"re-run from\n447\t  # the orchestrator's worktree\" silently means abandoning them. Report exactly\n448\t  # what is stranded and how to integrate it. Every command here is DIAGNOSTIC:\n449\t  # each is `|| true`-guarded so a failure degrades to the plain refusal above\n450\t  # rather than crashing before the message prints.\n451\t  _WT_BASE=\"\"\n452\t  for _ref in \"$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref --symbolic-full-name '@{u}' 2>/dev/null || true)\" \\\n453\t              origin/next origin/main next main; do\n454\t    [ -n \"$_ref\" ] || continue\n455\t    if git rev-parse --verify --quiet \"$_ref\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then _WT_BASE=\"$_ref\"; break; fi\n456\t  done\n457\t  _WT_AHEAD=\"\"\n458\t  [ -n \"$_WT_BASE\" ] && _WT_AHEAD=$(git rev-list --count \"$_WT_BASE..HEAD\" 2>/dev/null || true)\n459\t  # Count BEFORE truncating, so a long list reports its true size rather than\n460\t  # under-reporting what is stranded — which is the whole point of this report.\n461\t  _WT_DIRTY_ALL=$(git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null || true)\n462\t  _WT_DIRTY_N=0\n463\t  [ -n \"$_WT_DIRTY_ALL\" ] && _WT_DIRTY_N=$(printf '%s\\n' \"$_WT_DIRTY_ALL\" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\n464\t  _WT_HAS_COMMITS=0\n465\t  [ -n \"$_WT_AHEAD\" ] && [ \"$_WT_AHEAD\" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null && _WT_HAS_COMMITS=1\n466\t\n467\t  echo \"\" >&2\n468\t  echo \"── Handoff: what is in this worktree (#1856) ──\" >&2\n469\t  if [ \"$_WT_HAS_COMMITS\" -eq 1 ]; then\n470\t    echo \"  $_WT_AHEAD commit(s) on '$ORCH_BRANCH' not on '$_WT_BASE':\" >&2\n471\t    git log --oneline --no-decorate \"$_WT_BASE..HEAD\" 2>/dev/null | head -20 | sed 's/^/    /' >&2 || true\n472\t    [ \"$_WT_AHEAD\" -gt 20 ] 2>/dev/null && echo \"    … and $((_WT_AHEAD - 20)) more\" >&2\n473\t    echo \"  These live ONLY on this branch. Switching away without integrating loses them.\" >&2\n474\t  fi\n475\t  if [ -n \"$_WT_DIRTY_ALL\" ]; then\n476\t    echo \"  $_WT_DIRTY_N uncommitted change(s) still in this worktree:\" >&2\n477\t    printf '%s\\n' \"$_WT_DIRTY_ALL\" | head -20 | sed 's/^/    /' >&2\n478\t    [ \"$_WT_DIRTY_N\" -gt 20 ] 2>/dev/null && echo \"    … and $((_WT_DIRTY_N - 20)) more\" >&2\n479\t  fi\n480\t  if [ \"$_WT_HAS_COMMITS\" -eq 1 ] || [ -n \"$_WT_DIRTY_ALL\" ]; then\n481\t    echo \"\" >&2\n482\t    echo \"  To integrate before continuing:\" >&2\n483\t    [ -n \"$_WT_DIRTY_ALL\" ] && echo \"    1. git add -A && git commit -m 'wip: recover worktree state'   # from THIS worktree\" >&2\n484\t    echo \"    2. cd <orchestrator worktree>   # a checkout whose branch is NOT agent-*/worktree-agent-*\" >&2\n485\t    echo \"    3. git merge --no-ff $ORCH_BRANCH        # or: git cherry-pick <sha>...  for selected commits\" >&2\n486\t    echo \"    4. re-run the phase from there\" >&2\n487\t    echo \"  Verify with: git log --oneline ${_WT_BASE:-HEAD}..$ORCH_BRANCH\" >&2\n488\t  fi\n489\t  exit 1\n490\tfi\n491\t# Pin to the worktree root; each later orchestrator-side block re-pins the same way\n492\t# (see the #3174 cleanup guard). Treat $ORCHESTRATOR_WT as the canonical root for the\n493\t# rest of the phase — prefer `git -C \"$ORCHESTRATOR_WT\"` for cross-step git calls,\n494\t# since a bare `cd` does not persist across separate tool invocations.\n495\texport ORCHESTRATOR_WT\n496\tcd \"$ORCHESTRATOR_WT\" || { echo \"FATAL: cannot cd to orchestrator worktree '$ORCHESTRATOR_WT' (#48).\" >&2; exit 1; }\n497\t```\n498\t\n499\t**Stream-idle-timeout prevention — checkpoint heartbeats (#2410):**\n500\t\n501\tMulti-plan phases can accumulate enough subagent context that the Claude API\n502\tSSE layer terminates with `Stream idle timeout - partial response received`\n503\tbetween a large tool_result and the next assistant turn (seen on Claude Code\n504\t+ Opus 4.7 at ~200K+ cache_read). To keep the stream warm, emit short\n505\tassistant-text heartbeats — **no tool call, just a literal line** — at every\n506\twave and plan boundary. Each heartbeat MUST start with `[checkpoint]` so\n507\ttooling and `/gsd-manager`'s background-completion handler can grep partial\n508\ttranscripts. `{P}/{Q}` is the phase-wide completed/total plans counter and\n509\tincreases monotonically across waves. `{status}` is `complete` (success),\n510\t`failed` (executor error), or `checkpoint` (human-gate returned).\n511\t\n512\t```\n513\t[checkpoint] phase {PHASE_NUMBER} wave {N}/{M} starting, {wave_plan_count} plan(s), {P}/{Q} plans done\n514\t[checkpoint] phase {PHASE_NUMBER} wave {N}/{M} plan {plan_id} starting ({P}/{Q} plans done)\n515\t[checkpoint] phase {PHASE_NUMBER} wave {N}/{M} plan {plan_id} {status} ({P}/{Q} plans done)\n516\t[checkpoint] phase {PHASE_NUMBER} wave {N}/{M} complete, {P}/{Q} plans done ({wave_success}/{wave_plan_count} ok)\n517\t```\n518\t\n519\t**For each wave:**\n520\t\n521\t@~/.claude/gsd-core/references/execute-phase-wave-guard.md\n522\t\n523\t@~/.claude/gsd-core/references/execute-phase-context-guard.md\n524\t\n525\t1. **Intra-wave files_modified overlap check (BEFORE spawning):**\n526\t\n527\t   Before spawning any agents for this wave, inspect the `files_modified` list of all plans\n528\t   in the wave. Check every pair of plans in the wave — if any two plans share even one file\n529\t   in their `files_modified` lists, those plans have an implicit dependency and MUST NOT run\n530\t   in parallel.\n531\t\n532\t   **Detection algorithm (pseudocode):**\n533\t   ```\n534\t   seen_files = {}\n535\t   overlapping_plans = []\n536\t   for each plan in wave_plans:\n537\t     for each file in plan.files_modified:\n538\t       if file in seen_files:\n539\t         overlapping_plans.add(plan, seen_files[file])  # both plans overlap on this file\n540\t       else:\n541\t         seen_files[file] = plan\n542\t   ```\n543\t\n544\t   **If overlap is detected:**\n545\t   - Warn the user:\n546\t     ```\n547\t     ⚠ Intra-wave files_modified overlap detected in Wave {N}:\n548\t       Plan {A} and Plan {B} both modify {file}\n549\t       Running these plans sequentially to avoid parallel worktree conflicts.\n550\t     ```\n551\t   - Override `PARALLELIZATION` to `false` for this wave only — run all plans in the wave\n552\t     sequentially regardless of the global parallelization setting.\n553\t   - This is a safety net for plans that were incorrectly assigned to the same wave.\n554\t     The planner should have caught this; flag it as a planning defect so the user can\n555\t     replan the phase if desired.\n556\t\n557\t   **If no overlap:** proceed normally (parallel if `PARALLELIZATION=true`).\n558\t\n559\t2. **Describe what's being built (BEFORE spawning):**\n560\t\n561\t   **First, emit the wave-start checkpoint heartbeat as a literal assistant-text\n562\t   line — no tool call (#2410). Do NOT skip this even for single-plan waves; it\n563\t   is required before any further reasoning or spawning:**\n564\t\n565\t   ```\n566\t   [checkpoint] phase {PHASE_NUMBER} wave {N}/{M} starting, {wave_plan_count} plan(s), {P}/{Q} plans done\n567\t   ```\n568\t\n569\t   Then read each plan's `<objective>`. Extract what's being built and why.\n570\t\n571\t   ```\n572\t   ---\n573\t   ## Wave {N}\n574\t\n575\t   **{Plan ID}: {Plan Name}**\n576\t   {2-3 sentences: what this builds, technical approach, why it matters}\n577\t\n578\t   Spawning {count} agent(s)... (runs in a subagent — no output until it returns, ~1–5 min; expected, not a freeze)\n579\t   ---\n580\t   ```\n581\t\n582\t   - Bad: \"Executing terrain generation plan\"\n583\t   - Good: \"Procedural terrain generator using Perlin noise — creates height maps and biome zones. Required before vehicle physics.\"\n584\t\n585\t2.5. **Per-plan worktree decision (run for each plan in this wave BEFORE its dispatch):**\n586\t\n587\t   Read and execute `gsd-core/workflows/execute-phase/steps/per-plan-worktree-gate.md` for each plan. It extracts `PLAN_FILES` from the plan's JSON, intersects against `SUBMODULE_PATHS` (with normalization, bidirectional matching, and glob-prefix handling), and sets `USE_WORKTREES_FOR_PLAN` to `false` when the plan touches a submodule path. Append `plan_id` to a `WAVE_WORKTREE_PLANS` accumulator when `USE_WORKTREES_FOR_PLAN != false`.\n588\t\n589\t   The dispatch branches in step 3 gate on both `USE_WORKTREES` and `USE_WORKTREES_FOR_PLAN` (#2474).\n590\t\n591\t2.75. **Execute:wave:pre capability dispatch:**\n592\t\n593\t   ```bash\n594\t   WAVE_PRE_HOOKS_JSON=$(gsd_run loop render-hooks execute:wave:pre --raw)\n595\t   ```\n596\t\n597\t   **Contribution dispatch:** inject every `kind == \"contribution\"` fragment per @gsd-core/references/loop-hook-dispatch.md (skip when none); one naming an alternate wave dispatch replaces step 3's inline loop.\n598\t\n599\t   **Step dispatch:** `kind == \"step\"` per @gsd-core/references/loop-hook-dispatch.md; never blocks or redirects executor spawning. ⚠ Validate `ref.command` in-context before any shell use.\n600\t\n601\t3. **Spawn executor agents:**\n602\t\n603\t   **Emit a plan-start heartbeat (literal line, no tool call) immediately before\n604\t   each `Agent()` dispatch (#2410):**\n605\t\n606\t   `[checkpoint] phase {PHASE_NUMBER} wave {N}/{M} plan {plan_id} starting ({P}/{Q} plans done)`\n607\t\n608\t   Pass paths only — executors read files themselves.\n609\t\n610\t   **Substitute `{plan_id}` in the prompt below with this plan's `id` field** from the `phase-plan-index` JSON loaded in step 1 (the same field referred to elsewhere in this workflow as `plan.id`) — unmodified and un-truncated, never a paraphrase. The guard hooks compare this value verbatim against the sentinel the per-plan gate wrote (`per-plan-worktree-gate.md`'s `plan_id`); a paraphrase or an omission costs the dispatch its recorded isolation decision.\n611\t\n612\t   **Executor routing (#1689/#3370).** Per plan, run `gsd-core/workflows/execute-phase/steps/per-plan-executor-routing.md` to set `EXECUTOR_TYPE` for `subagent_type=\"{EXECUTOR_TYPE}\"` below.\n613\t\n614\t   **TDD-applicability resolution (#4266/#4272).** Run `gsd-core/workflows/execute-phase/steps/tdd-applicability-resolution.md`.\n615\t\n616\t   **Worktree mode** (`USE_WORKTREES` and `USE_WORKTREES_FOR_PLAN` not `false`):\n617\t\n618\t   Before spawning, capture the current HEAD:\n619\t   ```bash\n620\t   EXPECTED_BASE=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\n621\t   DISPATCH_TS=$(date -u +\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\")\n622\t   EXPECTED_BRANCH=$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)\n623\t    if [ \"${USE_WORKTREES:-true}\" != \"false\" ] && [ \"${USE_WORKTREES_FOR_PLAN:-true}\" != \"false\" ] && [ -z \"${WAVE_WORKTREE_MANIFEST:-}\" ]; then\n624\t     M=$(mktemp \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/gsd-worktree-wave-XXXXXX\") && mv \"$M\" \"$M.json\" && WAVE_WORKTREE_MANIFEST=\"$M.json\" || exit 1  # XXXXXX must be path-final on BSD/macOS (#1520)\n625\t     # Persist the dispatch-time orchestrator worktree root so wave-cleanup can pin back to the\n626\t     # orchestrator's OWN worktree — NOT `git worktree list`'s first entry (always the main\n627\t     # checkout), which pins a non-primary (per-phase lane) orchestrator off its branch (#630).\n628\t     # Dispatch runs from the orchestrator's lane, so show-toplevel here is the correct root.\n629\t     ORCH_ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)\n630\t     ORCH_ROOT=\"$ORCH_ROOT\" MANIFEST=\"$WAVE_WORKTREE_MANIFEST\" node -e 'const fs=require(\"fs\");fs.writeFileSync(process.env.MANIFEST,JSON.stringify({orchestrator_root:process.env.ORCH_ROOT||null,worktrees:[]})+\"\\n\")'\n631\t     export WAVE_WORKTREE_MANIFEST\n632\t   fi\n633\t   ```\n634\t\n635\t   **Isolation model.** The block below is the **`harness-worktree`** path. For `orchestrator-worktree` use the dispatch below it; for `none` use sequential mode. Both are detailed in `execute-phase/steps/executor-isolation-dispatch.md`.\n636\t\n637\t   **Sequential dispatch for parallel execution (waves with 2+ agents):**\n638\t   Dispatch each `Agent()` call **one at a time with `run_in_background: true`**. Do NOT\n639\t   send all Agent calls in a single message: simultaneous `git worktree add` calls race\n640\t   on `.git/config.lock`. Agents still run in parallel once their worktrees are created.\n641\t\n642\t   ```text\n643\t   # CORRECT: one Agent() per message with run_in_background: true\n644\t   # WRONG: multiple Agent() calls in one message -> .git/config.lock contention\n645\t   ```\n646\t\n647\t   ```text\n648\t   Agent(\n649\t    subagent_type=\"{EXECUTOR_TYPE}\",\n650\t    description=\"Execute plan {plan_number} of phase {phase_number}\",\n651\t     # Only include model= when executor_model is an explicit model name.\n652\t     # When executor_model is \"inherit\", omit this parameter entirely so\n653\t     # Claude Code inherits the orchestrator model automatically.\n654\t     model=\"{executor_model}\",  # omit this line when executor_model == \"inherit\"\n655\t     # The host's OWN declared isolation flag (`harnessFlag` from\n656\t     # `dispatch-isolation --json`; see the isolation-dispatch fragment).\n657\t     # Emit the declared token — do NOT hardcode a runtime's flag.\n658\t     {harnessFlag},\n659\t     prompt=\"\n660\t       <objective>\n661\t       Execute plan {plan_number} of phase {phase_number}-{phase_name}.\n662\t       [gsd:dispatch phase=\"{phase_number}\" plan=\"{plan_id}\"]\n663\t       Commit each task atomically. Create SUMMARY.md.\n664\t       Do NOT update STATE.md or ROADMAP.md — the orchestrator owns those writes after all worktree agents in the wave complete.\n665\t       </objective>\n666\t\n667\t       <worktree_branch_check>\n668\t       ORCHESTRATOR build-time embed (NOT a sub-agent runtime step): before this dispatch, read `gsd-core/references/worktree-branch-check.md`, substitute `{EXPECTED_BASE}` with the base SHA captured above ({EXPECTED_BASE}), and replace this note with that fragment's `<worktree_branch_check>` block so the dispatched prompt carries the runnable guard verbatim — do not pass this instruction through in its place.\n669\t       Per-commit HEAD/cwd-drift/path-guard: `agents/gsd-executor.md` steps 0/0a/0b + `gsd-core/references/worktree-path-safety.md` (in <execution_context>).\n670\t       </worktree_branch_check>\n671\t\n672\t       <parallel_execution>\n673\t       You are running as a PARALLEL executor agent in a git worktree. Worktree path safety (cwd-drift, absolute-path guards) is in `worktree-path-safety.md` (loaded below).\n674\t       Run `git commit` normally — hooks run by default. Do NOT pass `--no-verify`\n675\t       unless the orchestrator surfaces `workflow.worktree_skip_hooks=true` in this\n676\t       prompt; silent bypass violates project CLAUDE.md guidance (#2924).\n677\t\n678\t       IMPORTANT: Do NOT modify STATE.md or ROADMAP.md. execute-plan.md\n679\t       auto-detects worktree mode (`.git` is a file, not a directory) and skips\n680\t       shared file updates automatically. The orchestrator updates them centrally\n681\t       after merge.\n682\t\n683\t       REQUIRED: SUMMARY.md MUST be committed before you return. In worktree mode the\n684\t       git_commit_metadata step in execute-plan.md commits SUMMARY.md and REQUIREMENTS.md\n685\t       only (STATE.md and ROADMAP.md are excluded automatically). Do NOT skip or defer\n686\t       this commit — the orchestrator force-removes the worktree after you return, and\n687\t       any uncommitted SUMMARY.md will be permanently lost (#2070).\n688\t       REQUIRED ORDER: Write SUMMARY.md → commit → only then any narration. No text between Write and commit (truncation risk; #2070 rescue is not primary defense).\n689\t\n690\t       </parallel_execution>\n691\t\n692\t       <execution_context>\n693\t       ORCHESTRATOR build-time embed (NOT a sub-agent runtime step): before this dispatch, read each file listed below and replace this note with those files' contents, inlined verbatim in this block in the listed order. Never leave `@`-include lines in the dispatched prompt — `@path` never expands inside an Agent() `prompt=\"...\"` string (#3324), so an include arrives as literal text the executor never sees.\n694\t       - `$HOME/.claude/gsd-core/workflows/execute-plan.md`\n695\t       - `$HOME/.claude/gsd-core/templates/summary.md`\n696\t       - `$HOME/.claude/gsd-core/references/checkpoints.md`\n697\t       ${TDD_APPLICABLE ? '- `$HOME/.claude/gsd-core/references/tdd.md`' : ''}  # #3990/#4265: type: tdd, tdd=\"true\", or workflow.tdd_mode\n698\t       - `$HOME/.claude/gsd-core/references/worktree-path-safety.md`\n699\t       ${CONTEXT_WINDOW < 200000 ? '' : '- `$HOME/.claude/gsd-core/references/executor-examples.md`'}\n700\t       </execution_context>\n701\t\n702\t       <required_reading>\n703\t       Read these files at execution start using the Read tool.\n704\t       First resolve repo root so every path is anchored:\n705\t       \\`PROJECT_ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)\\`\n706\t       - ${PROJECT_ROOT}/{phase_dir}/{plan_file} (Plan)\n707\t       - ${PROJECT_ROOT}/.planning/PROJECT.md (Project context — core value, requirements, evolution rules)\n708\t       - ${PROJECT_ROOT}/.planning/STATE.md (State)\n709\t       - ${PROJECT_ROOT}/.planning/config.json (Config, if exists)\n710\t       ${CONTEXT_WINDOW >= 500000 ? `\n711\t       - ${PROJECT_ROOT}/${phase_dir}/*-CONTEXT.md (User decisions from discuss-phase — honors locked choices)\n712\t       - ${PROJECT_ROOT}/${phase_dir}/*-RESEARCH.md (Technical research — pitfalls and patterns to follow)\n713\t       - ${PROJECT_ROOT}/${prior_wave_summaries} (SUMMARY.md files from earlier waves in this phase — what was already built)\n714\t       ` : ''}\n715\t       - ${PROJECT_ROOT}/CLAUDE.md (Project instructions, if exists —